}{\fs22 \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 As I write this I can\rquote t help thinking how \lquote clairvoyant\rquote  Apache has become over the years.  Mind you, it\rquote s clairvoyant abilities stem from the hard work and forward thinking of the Apache Group and the many talented contributors. \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  Maybe I am just biased in my opinion, but I would have to say that as the years have unfolded Apache\rquote s overall performance and functionality has kept pace, if not surpassed, my expectations of what I believe a web server should accomplish in the business\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 world.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 I first \lquote \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 utilized\rquote \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  Apache in 1995 when I was installing a new AIX RS-6000 8-way.  The project I was working on needed a better way to distribute documents to remote locations.  Those locations were all dial-ups (28.8K) and the need to share inform\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 ation was a considerable one.  The remote sites were all using OS/2 Warp 3 Connect and basic office tasks were completed using a slew of Microsoft and similar products.  At that time there were no real multi-platform document sharing systems available.  N\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 o\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 t unless you wanted to spend big money \endash  which wasn\rquote t an option as this was a one-man operation, one which had to keep 500+ users spread-out over 900 square miles at 15 remote locations happy.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 I had heard about this thing call open source technology \endash  bac\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 k then more commonly known as freeware for Unix systems \endash  and was searching archives at CompuServe, Delphi, many ftp sites, even using gopher to root out anything possible to help me with solving some pressing problems.  \par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 There wasn\rquote t a whole lot to go on\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  and I was about to prepare a proposal to ask for more money.  Then, as luck would have it, late one afternoon I found an ftp server with a copy of Apache v0.95 for AIX.  I downloaded it and uncompressed it; it appeared dismal and uninteresting.  I was ve\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 r\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 y unsure whether it would even work much less do what I needed it to accomplish.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 After kicking it around for about an hour I got it working.  Then the real fun began \endash  I began to realize I didn\rquote t know TCP/IP as well as I thought I did.  During that week A\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 pache showed me ways to think about Unix and TCP/IP I hadn\rquote t before.  OK, I\rquote ll give \endash  Apache itself didn\rquote t, but the work going into getting that first version working was a major leap forward in my systems administration skills and Unix networking knowled\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 g\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 e.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 It was a bear.  Days of installing \endash  configuring \endash  testing \endash  downloading a new version \endash  installing \endash  configuring \endash \hich\f20  testing\u8230\'c9\loch\f20   You get the picture, I am sure.  It reminded me of back when I briefly looked at Linux and decided it wasn\rquote t ready for commerc\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 ial primetime just yet.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 After about two weeks of soul searching, sleepless research, and wondering if this was a bad idea all around \endash  I implemented a distributed document sharing system which allowed all the remote sites to share their reports and other \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 data in almost real-time.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 The system simply used custom and automated FTP, telnet, and remote printing to accomplish the following tasks:\par \par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af20\hich\af20\dbch\f4 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 1)\tab}}\pard \fi-800\li800\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx800{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls4\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent800\pnhang{\pntxta )}}\ls4\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Using FTP make daily reports available for review by peer site;\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af20\hich\af20\dbch\f4 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 2)\tab}}\pard \fi-800\li800\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx800{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls4\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent800\pnhang{\pntxta )}}\ls4\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Using automated telnet check availability of remote \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 systems and print servers;\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af20\hich\af20\dbch\f4 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 3)\tab}}\pard \fi-800\li800\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx800{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls4\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent800\pnhang{\pntxta )}}\ls4\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Using remote printing (sometimes a tty session hooked into a modem which was simply attached to a dot-matrix or laser printer) send out pre-formatted reports to be printed.  This saved having to drive everywhere on a daily basis \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 \hich\f20 just to drop off a report they were probably going to throw-out or recycle anyway\u8230\'c9\par }\pard \ri-720\widctlpar\adjustright {\fs22 \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Pretty simple and straight forward.  OK, you say, where does Apache come into this picture?  That\rquote s the beauty of it \endash  the last option, 3 above, was replaced by giving the \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 client sites the ability to pick and choose what reports they wanted delivered electronically or printed locally \endash  just by using a thing called a browser.  Simplistic? Yes, but in defense of myself and the project, upper management was scared to death abo\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 u\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 t the Internet. Anything I could do to show the power of direct, simple, solutions available from utilizing Internet related products would help dissuade their fears and was a blessing, in the scheme of things.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Thankfully, today installation and configura\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 tion of Apache is very well documented and generally intuitive.  Apache developers and Webmasters just starting out will not have to go through the \lquote guessing\rquote  game in getting Apache \endash  nor any other substantial open source system \endash  operational.  Plus, with\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 the wide range of tools, modules, and OPE (other people\rquote s experience) you and/or your team should become productive in short order.\par \par \par }\pard\plain \s18\ri-720\widctlpar\adjustright \b\fs32\loch\af20\hich\af20\dbch\f4\cgrid {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Business practice:\line \par }\pard\plain \ri-720\widctlpar\adjustright \loch\af4\hich\af4\dbch\f4\cgrid {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 As time progressed I researched Apache further and found that it is a \lquote collaborative\rquote  project; softwar\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 e built by the individual efforts of many people.  At first this was hard to believe, so I wrote out an e-mail to the Apache Group, asking them about this \lquote effort\rquote  and why people would be willing to put forth such effort for free?\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 I was concerned about se\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 curity, product deliverables, product enhancement, and survivability in today\rquote s dynamic computing market.  Things that some of you may not have heard of; mainly because your development environment may be directed and dictated by other departments in your\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 business \endash  be it a small company, large firm, public facility, or educational institution.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 I was interested in these things because in the business environments I was currently in, and had been in the past, products folded up and disappeared within a mont\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 h.  No, I\rquote m not talking about a mom-n-pop software operation \endash  I speak of entire operating systems which had been in use at large agencies for decades become obsolete and were replaced in less than a year.  Where entire careers were made or destroyed beca\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 u\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 se you picked the wrong software. I came from a background where no one was ever fired for choosing IBM \endash  and then IBM lays off 40 thousand in one shot.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 No, whatever I did with computers and software, I planned on staying around long after the smoke clear\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 ed.  I was very interested in the long-term life of the solution I had just built and implemented and needed to know of any alternate paths to take once it started breaking-down, if that was normal and to be expected with this thing called \lquote open source.\rquote  \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 I mean, really, if it did break substantially, who would I call for help?\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 But I digress.  Not really expecting a reply, I was pleasantly surprised to hear back from Brian with-in the week (not that he took that long to reply, but that I didn\rquote t check my In\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 ternet e-mail that often when I was working on such a big project.\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 )\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 The explanation given was that their efforts were free only in the sense that people using Apache paid no money for it.  However, it was customary for other developers to \lquote donate\rquote  their ef\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 forts back into the project so that all could benefit.  There\endash in lies the \lquote pay\rquote  for the \lquote work\rquote  on such a project.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Today, current examples of open source projects that revolve around Apache are Jakarta, XML-Apache, mod_perl, mod_js, and mod_php, just to na\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 me a few.  I mention these here because these projects represent the primary strengths of Apache.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 I read an article in the Jan 10}{\fs22\super \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 th}{\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  issue of ComputerWorld in which it was stated that APIs are what make the computer programming world go round.  It basicall\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 y stated that APIs were better than \lquote open\rquote  or \lquote closed\rquote  architectures because it provided a defined interface with which to interact with the project software (whatever software you may be developing in your current project.)\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 The analogy they provided for \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 the \lquote open\rquote  architecture was one where someone could, at anytime, walk into your yard and fiddle with your lawn mower; making whatever changes were required to get the mower to perform best in your yard.  Your yard in this case being whatever operating env\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 i\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 ronment and/or application software environment your business may be using.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 This analogy, while on the surface appears sound, has a few problems.\par \par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af20\hich\af20\dbch\f4 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 A)\tab}}\pard \fi-720\li720\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls6\pnrnot0\pnucltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta )}}\ls6\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 The lawn mower Apache owns doesn\rquote t get changed while you are making your enhancements.  What really happens \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 is - you get a \lquote copy\rquote  of the project \endash  which you make changes to.  This is called code base \lquote splitting\rquote  and is a common practice in the software development business.  Otherwise developers would keep having to reinvent the wheel each time they wanted to d\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 r\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 \hich\f20 ive to the store to get a Mountain Dew\u174\'a8\loch\f20  fix }{\fs22 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 74 \\f "Wingdings" \\s 11}{\fldrslt\f14\fs22}}}{\fs22 \par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af20\hich\af20\dbch\f4 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 B)\tab}}\pard \fi-720\li720\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls6\pnrnot0\pnucltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta )}}\ls6\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Once done, you can use what you have made \endash  free \endash  in your own business.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af20\hich\af20\dbch\f4 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 C)\tab}}\pard \fi-720\li720\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls6\pnrnot0\pnucltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta )}}\ls6\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 If, after awhile, you see that these changes would be beneficial to others - you ask the Apache Software Foundation to review what you\rquote ve\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  done and whether they would make good additions to the Apache project.  You should not simply distribute your \lquote enhancements\rquote  without prior approval.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af20\hich\af20\dbch\f4 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 D)\tab}}\pard \fi-720\li720\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls6\pnrnot0\pnucltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxta )}}\ls6\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 The original lawn mower is still in original condition \endash  ready for the next person to stop by and \lquote borrow \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 it.\rquote \par }\pard \ri-720\widctlpar\adjustright {\fs22 \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 However, there is one aspect of the ComputerWorld article that remains true \endash  if you change the source code \endash  you are now in the software maintenance business.  One of the foremost strengths of Apache is the modularity of design and the number of pro\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 ject contributors who design various modules.  In the Additional Resources section, at the end of this document, I will provide a listing of places you can gather more information from on the Internet.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 APIs are a \lquote good thing\rquote , no doubt, but sometimes you \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 really need to get at the root of the problem and to do that you will need the source code.  Apache provides a wonderful API.  OK, maybe it\rquote s not as great as it could be, but it is improving with every release; just as well maintained and supported softwa\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 r\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 e should be.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Apache web server software is treated just like, if not better than, most high-end mainstream software.  There is a panel of experts who review source code changes and enhancements; there are support mailing lists in which you can discuss thi\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 ngs about Apache.  After spending several years working with big companies trying to workout costs, fees, product direction, and similar issues; I find Apache most welcome and a refreshing change of pace.  One of those open source projects which has a fee\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 l\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  of professional business attire without all the snooty my product is better than your product and \ldblquote I\rquote ll make you pay dearly to prove it\rdblquote .\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 OK, a word of warning.  Free doesn\rquote t mean without costs.  You will need to put forth effort in your Apache related p\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 rojects.  Effort which cost\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 s\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  you and/or your company time and money.  Effort you could have spent pursuing other things.  You will need to get past the \ldblquote I bought it \endash  therefore I have a right to know when I can get an updated version which fixes those bugs \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 I mentioned.\rdblquote   Bug troubleshooting and repair in the Apache environment progresses and it progress quite well.  The caveat is that the players are volunteers and unless the particular bug you reported was a \lquote show stopper\rquote  \endash  it is unlikely to get fixed any\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 faster than any other reported by your peers.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 In all my professional business experience using Apache I have yet to see a show stopper which prevented me from accomplishing the task at hand.  Usually the only thing that stops me is my inexperience in desi\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 gning appropriate solutions when I am thrust into a new environment \endash  everyone needs time to learn to swim again.\par \par \par }{\b\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Obtaining funding and/or upper management support:}{\fs22 \line \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Apache is one of those projects where you get a big return on a little investment.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 The \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 approach that I have found successful is one that involves the following:\par \par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af3\hich\af3\dbch\f4 \loch\af3\dbch\af4\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls7\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Identify an unmet need (or an area that could use improvement) \endash  let\rquote s say customer feedback.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af3\hich\af3\dbch\f4 \loch\af3\dbch\af4\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls7\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Research what you feel is available in your current development environment with which \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 to meet this need.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af3\hich\af3\dbch\f4 \loch\af3\dbch\af4\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls7\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Assemble the pieces together and develop a test environment to see if is feasible.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af3\hich\af3\dbch\f4 \loch\af3\dbch\af4\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls7\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Once you get all the right parts together to accomplish the task at hand \endash  write a report calculating what each one costs you.  Include things like all th\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 e product types and purchase prices (whether this includes per user or per sever fees, etc.)  How long it took to make the test environment \endash  whether any other designer time or input from other departments is wanted or required.  How many hours you spent \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 o\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 n the test development system, etc.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af3\hich\af3\dbch\f4 \loch\af3\dbch\af4\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls7\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Take that figure and multiply it by 5, add thirty percent for overhead and the final figure is what the project would cost to get off the ground and running for at least a year.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af3\hich\af3\dbch\f4 \loch\af3\dbch\af4\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls7\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Now, using that figure, calculate annual c\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 osts by adding software upgrades, training costs, and unforeseen maintenance and operational costs.\par {\pntext\pard\plain\fs22\loch\af3\hich\af3\dbch\f4 \loch\af3\dbch\af4\hich\f3 \u-3913\'b7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\ri-720\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls7\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls7\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 This is the true figure you would need to pitch to the upper management team and to the budget keepers \endash  although if you do get CEO-level support I doubt an\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 yone will say no \endash  but that is a topic for another class.\par }\pard \ri-720\widctlpar\adjustright {\fs22 \par \par }\pard\plain \s18\ri-720\widctlpar\adjustright \b\fs32\loch\af20\hich\af20\dbch\f4\cgrid {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Project development:\par }\pard\plain \ri-720\widctlpar\adjustright \loch\af4\hich\af4\dbch\f4\cgrid {\fs22 \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Alright; let\rquote s cover a real world case:\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 We needed to create a customer order/feedback system which revolved around inventory \endash  doesn\rquote t matter what kind of inventory, let\rquote s s\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 ay pizza \endash  pizza? just bear with me.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Pizza is made of up of separate, individually orderable parts \endash  pepperoni, cheese, meat sauce, lettuce (OK, not lettuce), olives, ham, bacon, bar-b-que (seriously.)  Things like that.  Each of these items has real valu\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 e and costs the company X number of dollars to prepare and place on any given pizza.  And, to make matters worse, not everyone orders the same types of pizza regularly, so it is sometimes difficult to have all the needed ingredients on hand when needed.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 S\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 o, how do we find out what items we need on-hand at any given time.  We could still use the inventory tracking system \endash  but that would only say what was being used not really what areas of town were buying pizzas; and we are interested in where our pizzas\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 are going as well as who was purchasing them. So, we design a customer order/feedback form \endash  web enabled \endash  asking the following information:\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Name\par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Address with Zip code\par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Phone number with Area Code\par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Number of pizzas.\par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Any comments?\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 They submit this\par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 form as \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 filled-out\par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 \hich\f20 thus far\u8230\'c9\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 The server creates another individual form for each pizza which they wish to order, asking for the following \endash \par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 For Pizza Number X:\par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Please pick size \endash  (s, m, l, x)\par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Please pick toppings: (Shift-Click to select multiple toppings)\par \tab \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Peppe\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 roni\par \tab \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Lettuce\par }\pard \fi720\ri-720\widctlpar\adjustright {\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 (Clients ask for this stuff, go figure!)\par }\pard \ri-720\widctlpar\adjustright {\fs22 \tab \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Bar-B-Que\par \tab \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Onions\par \tab \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Green Olives\par \tab \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Black Olives\par \tab \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Sardines\par \tab \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Kidney beans\par \tab \loch\af20\dbch\af4\hich\f20 \u8230\'c9\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Please enter any additional toppings not listed above:\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Please indicate any special preparation instructions:\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Please indicate\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  any special directions about how to find your home or business location:\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 NOTE:  Do you want the remaining pizzas made the same as this one?\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 They submit this\par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 \hich\f20 form as filled-out\u8230\'c9\par \par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 The server looks up whether or not the items desired are actually availab\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 le; if so, submits a job order placing these items on hold (and unavailable for another customer \endash  this prevents more than one customer getting the last head of lettuce }{\fs22 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 74 \\f "Wingdings" \\s 11}{\fldrslt\f14\fs22}}}{\fs22 \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 .)\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 The server decides whether to make all the rest of the order the same, if not, it \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 sends out the form again asking for another pizza.\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 So on, and so forth, until the order(s) are tallied up, payment is either authorized or indicated, and the individual pizzas ordered (if more than one) are all put back together as a single order and sent\hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20  out to be created and finally delivered.  Sounds yummy already!\par \par \hich\af20\dbch\af4\loch\f20 Now, all this could be done using cookies (client or server) or, after having them sign up, they could simply be picked out of a database and asked if the